    "result": "I've successfully fixed the tax rounding issue in the invoicing system. \n\n## Problem\nThe tax calculations were incorrect - specifically, 5% tax on $2.50 (12.5 cents) was rounding down to 12 cents instead of up to 13 cents as expected.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe `round_half_up` function in `invoicing/money.py` was using Python's built-in `round()` function which implements \"banker's rounding\" (round half to even), causing 12.5 to round to 12 instead of 13.\n\n## Solution\nI updated the `round_half_up` function to properly implement \"round half up\" behavior where ties round away from zero:\n- 12.5 now correctly rounds to 13 (instead of 12)\n- -2.5 now correctly rounds to -3 (instead of -2)\n\n## Verification\n- \u2705 The specific failing test now passes\n- \u2705 All 85 tests in the test suite pass (previously 1 was failing)\n- \u2705 No regressions introduced\n- \u2705 The fix handles both positive and negative rounding cases correctly\n\nThe fix is minimal and targeted, addressing exactly the rounding behavior needed without any side effects. Tax calculations now correctly round 12.5 cents up to 13 cents as required by the test specifications.",
